<div class="adv5">

  <!-- ARTICLE HEADER -->
  <article class="wrap adv5-art">
    <div class="adv5-cat">Inside the GLP-1 Boom</div>
    <h1 class="adv5-h1">The new supplement people are quietly calling <em>"nature's Ozempic."</em></h1>
    <p class="adv5-deck">You take it with your morning coffee, and the people using it say they finally stopped thinking about food all day. Here's why a growing number of would-be GLP-1 patients are trying this first.</p>
    <div class="adv5-meta">
      <span>By <b>The Absorption Company</b></span>
      <span class="adv5-meta__dot"></span>
      <span>{{ pub_date }}</span>
      <span class="adv5-meta__read">7 min read</span>
    </div>

    <!-- LEAD IMAGE -->
    <figure class="adv5-lead">
      <img src="{{ hero_img }}" alt="WLP-1 by The Absorption Company" loading="eager">
      <figcaption class="adv5-lead__cap"><b>WLP-1</b> by The Absorption Company. Two capsules with morning coffee.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <!-- BODY -->
    <div class="adv5-body">

      <p class="lead">The waiting room is fuller than it used to be. So is the prescription queue.</p>

      <p>GLP-1 injections went from a niche diabetes drug to the most-asked-about weight loss tool in America. Most of the people asking aren't 100 pounds overweight. They've got <b>~20 to ~40</b><span class="adv5-ast">*</span> they'd like gone, and the apps, the macros, and the 5am workouts didn't stick.</p>

      <p>So they made the appointment. Then they read the fine print: <b>$1,000+ a month</b>, weekly injections, nausea, gallbladder issues, and the slack, hollowed-out face the internet now calls <i>"Ozempic face."</i> Stop the drug, most of the weight comes back.</p>

      <p>A lot of them are walking out without filling the script. Not because they don't want the result. Because they're not sure they want it <i>like that</i>.</p>

      <h2>Enter "nature's Ozempic"</h2>

      <p>That hesitation is where a new phrase came in.</p>

      <p><b>WLP-1</b> is a supplement built by <b>The Absorption Company</b> in New York. Three patented plant extracts, two capsules with morning coffee, designed to target the same biology a GLP-1 injection targets. No prescription. No needle. No rebound.</p>

